    <li class="priority-high"><strong>C programming: Control Structures</strong> — Loops (for, while, do-while) and decision-making (if-else, switch, break/continue) appear in virtually every paper. Nested loops and loop equivalence conversions are particularly common in Short questions.</li>
    <li class="priority-high"><strong>Operators &amp; Data Types</strong> — Questions on arithmetic, relational, logical, and assignment operators appear yearly. Data type sizes, format specifiers, and operator precedence are MCQ staples.</li>
    <li class="priority-med"><strong>Functions &amp; Recursion</strong> — Function prototypes, return statements, parameter passing, and recursion appear in most years, especially in Long questions. Students should be prepared to write recursive functions.</li>
    <li class="priority-med"><strong>MS Access Objects</strong> — Table, Form, Report, Query, and Macro appear regularly in MCQs. Understanding the purpose of each object is essential for the objective section.</li>
    <li class="priority-med"><strong>Input/Output Functions</strong> — printf(), scanf(), format specifiers, and escape sequences are perennial MCQ and Short question topics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Question-Type Strategy</h3>
  <ul class="insight-list">
    <li class="priority-high"><strong>MCQs</strong> — Focus on definitions, sizes of data types, operator symbols, and MS Access terminology. MCQs test breadth of knowledge; rapid recall of standard facts (e.g., %d for int, &amp;&amp; for AND, 4 bytes for float) is critical.</li>
    <li class="priority-high"><strong>Short Questions</strong> — These frequently require code writing (simple programs), output prediction, and concept differentiation. Practice writing concise C programs for common tasks (area calculations, tables, loops, swaps) and tracing loop outputs.</li>
    <li class="priority-med"><strong>Long Questions</strong> — These combine multiple sub-topics. Each Long question typically includes one C programming sub-part and one DBMS/theory sub-part. Prepare structured explanations for: data types, database models, control structures, and MS Access objects.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Emerging Trends (2023–2025)</h3>
  <ul class="insight-list">
    <li>Recent papers (2024–2025) introduce questions on <strong>pointers, structures, unions, bitwise operators, and binary search</strong> — topics that were absent or rare in earlier years. This suggests a gradual expansion of the examined syllabus.</li>
